* Why functional programming is better - Sorting
- A sorting algorithm needs to be able to compare two elements. This is done via a comparator function.
- Before functional programming was a thing in Java, we had to provide a Comparator implementation via inheritance.
Java (unlearn this):
.play ../src/functional/genericstreams/sort/SortDemo.java /class/,

* Why functional programming is better - Sorting
- With functional programming, we can use a lambda expression to provide a comparator function.
.play ../src/functional/genericstreams/sort/stream/main.go /array/,/fmt.Println/

* Why is it better? Two core ideas

*1.* *Behavior* *as* *a* *parameter*

- The library owns the loop, you only say *what* should happen
- So the library can change *how* it happens: sequentially, in parallel, lazily, distributed

*2.* *Pure* *functions*

- The result depends only on the arguments
- You can understand, test, cache and parallelize a function on its own, without knowing the rest of the program

: Die erste Idee haben wir gerade gesehen (Filter, Sortieren). Die zweite Idee kommt im Teil "Pure Functions".

* Why now?

Why did C# (2007), C++ (2011) and Java (2014) add lambdas?

- *Multicore:* Clock speeds stopped rising around 2005 ("The free lunch is over"). If the library owns the loop, it can spread the work over all cores. You hand over the loop body as a function, and it must not touch shared state.
- *Big* *data:* Google's MapReduce (2004) showed that map and reduce scale to thousands of machines
- *Competition:* JavaScript, Ruby, Python and Scala showed how convenient functions as values are
- *Boilerplate:* Anonymous classes for every callback and comparator were too verbose

: "The Free Lunch Is Over" - Herb Sutter, 2005. Die Taktfrequenzen stagnieren seit etwa 2004 bei 3-4 GHz.
: Pure functions gab es schon immer (z. B. Math.sin). Neu ist, dass man der Bibliothek Verhalten übergibt (interne statt externe Iteration). Purity ist nur die Voraussetzung, damit die Bibliothek parallelisieren darf.
: LINQ und Java Streams sind die Folge, nicht der Grund: Sie kamen im selben Release wie die Lambdas.
: Hadoop nutzte noch keine Lambdas, sondern Mapper- und Reducer-Klassen. MapReduce zeigt, dass das Modell trägt.
: Am Ende der Vorlesung kommen wir mit Word Count und dem parallelen Reduce wieder hierher zurück.

