* What does this print? The answer

    0 1 2      (since Go 1.22)
    3 3 3      (up to Go 1.21)

- A closure captures the *variable*, not its value
- Up to Go 1.21, all three closures shared the same loop variable `i`. After the loop it is 3.
- Since Go 1.22, every iteration gets a new variable `i`
- A language changed the semantics of its loops, because this bug was so common

: Die Go-Version im go.mod entscheidet. Mit "go 1.21" im go.mod gibt das Programm 3 3 3 aus.
: JavaScript hat dasselbe Problem mit var. Mit let bekommt jede Iteration eine eigene Variable.

* JavaScript: this is not captured

#lang javascript

    const object1 = {
        prop: 42,

        func: function () {
            return this.prop;
       },
    };

    console.log(object1.prop)  // 42
    console.log(object1.func())  // 42  // At call time, this points to object1

    const object2 = {
        prop: 43,
        func: object1.func  // take the function defined in object1.
    }

    console.log(object2.prop)  // 43
    console.log(object2.func())  // 42 or 43?

: https://onecompiler.com/javascript
: object1.func = object1.func.bind(object1);

*

: Leere Folie, damit die Lösung nicht schon am Rand zu sehen ist.

* JavaScript: this is not captured. The answer

    43

- `this` is not part of the closure. It is bound at *call* time to the object before the dot.
- A classic bug: pass a method as callback, and `this` is lost

#lang javascript

    setTimeout(object1.func, 100)            // this is not object1
    setTimeout(object1.func.bind(object1), 100) // fixed with bind
    setTimeout(() => object1.func(), 100)       // fixed with an arrow function

- Arrow functions do not have their own `this`. They capture it from the surrounding code like a variable.

