* Back to Go: Functional Programming in Practice

* Recap: Functions as First Class Citizens in Go
- Go supports functions as 1st Class Citizens: Closures and Lambdas
- Functions can be assigned to variables
- Functions can be used as function parameters and return values (Higher-Order Functions)
- Functions can be created inside functions
- The Go standard library uses functional constructs

* Sample from the Go Standard Library
- strings.Map

#lang go

    // Map returns a copy of the string s with all its characters modified
    // according to the mapping function. If mapping returns a negative value, the character is
    // dropped from the string with no replacement.
    func Map(mapping func(rune) rune, s string) string

- Usage

#lang go

    s := "Hello, world!"
    s = strings.Map(func(r rune) rune {
        return r + 1
    }, s)
    fmt.Println(s) // --> Ifmmp-!xpsme"

* Map, Filter and Reduce

The three most important higher-order functions. Each takes a function as argument.

#lang text

    [1, 2, 3, 4]
        │  Map(x => x * x)         apply a function to every element
        ▼
    [1, 4, 9, 16]
        │  Filter(x => x is even)  keep only the elements that match
        ▼
    [4, 16]
        │  Reduce((a, b) => a + b) combine all elements into one value
        ▼
    20

- No loop, no index, no mutable variable in your code. The loop is in the library.

