* Exercise 5.3 - Map / Filter / Reduce

.link https://github.com/s-macke/concepts-of-programming-languages/blob/master/docs/exercises/Exercise5.md#exercise-53---map--filter--reduce Exercise 5.3
Map/Reduce is a famous functional construct implemented in many parallel and distributed collection frameworks like Hadoop, Apache Spark, Java Streams (not distributed but parallel), C# Linq

- Implement a custom M/R API with the following interface:

#lang go

    type Stream interface {
        Map(m Mapper) Stream
        Filter(p Predicate) Stream
        Reduce(a Accumulator) Any
    }

- What is the type of Mapper, Predicate and Accumulator?
- How can you make the types generic, so they work for any type, not only for string?

* Mapper, Predicate and Accumulator with any

.code ../src/functional/streams/streams.go /EXERCISE 5.3 START OMIT/,/EXERCISE 5.3 END OMIT/

- Works for every type, but needs type assertions like `o.(string)` at runtime

* Mapper, Predicate and Accumulator with Generics

Since Go 1.18, the types can be generic. The compiler checks the types.

#lang go

    // Mapper function maps a value of type T to a value of type U.
    type Mapper[T, U any] func(T) U

    // Predicate function returns true if a given element should be kept.
    type Predicate[T any] func(T) bool

    // Accumulator function returns a combined element.
    type Accumulator[T any] func(T, T) T

- Limitation: Methods in Go cannot have their own type parameters. `Map` from `T` to `U` must be a function, not a method of `Stream[T]`.

* The problem: Count the words of the whole web

- Google in 2004: Billions of web pages, thousands of machines
- How often does each word occur?
- With one shared map: every machine writes into the same map. It must be locked, and the machines wait for each other.
- Idea: Every machine counts its part on its own with pure functions. At the end, the partial results are combined.
- This is MapReduce, the "Why now?" from the beginning of the lecture

: Problem zuerst, dann die Übung. Die Lösung mit Map und Reduce ist Übung 5.4.

* Exercise 5.4 - Word Count (WC)

.link https://github.com/s-macke/concepts-of-programming-languages/blob/master/docs/exercises/Exercise5.md#exercise-54---word-count-wc Exercise 5.4

Word Count is a famous algorithm for demonstrating the power of distributed collections and functional programming. Word Count counts how often a word (string) occurs in a collection. It is easy to address that problem with shared state (a map), but this solution does not scale well. The question here is how to use a pure functional algorithm to enable parallel and distributed execution.

After running Word Count, you should get the following result:

#lang text

    INPUT:  []any{"a", "a", "b", "b", "D", "a"}
    OUTPUT: "a:3, b:2, D:1, "

Questions

- How can you implement the problem with the already built Map()/Filter()/Reduce() functions?
- Write an Unit Test to prove that your solution works as expected!

* Classic Word Count Sample
.code ../src/functional/streams/streams_test.go /wordcount/,/ENDWC OMIT/

* Your guess: Which accumulators can run in parallel?

Reduce splits the data into parts, reduces each part on its own core and combines the results.

#lang go

    sum := func(a, b int) int { return a + b }
    max := func(a, b int) int { if a > b { return a }; return b }
    sub := func(a, b int) int { return a - b }
    concat := func(a, b string) string { return a + "," + b }

: Raten lassen, dann weiterklicken.

*

: Leere Folie, damit die Lösung nicht schon am Rand zu sehen ist.

* Which accumulators can run in parallel? The answer

- `sum`, `max`, `concat`: yes, they are *associative*: (a+b)+c = a+(b+c)
- `sub`: no. (8-4)-2 = 2, but 8-(4-2) = 6
- `concat` is not commutative: the parts must be combined in the right order
- An associative accumulator without shared state is the key for parallel and distributed execution

: Überleitung zu den Abschlussfragen und zu Vorlesung Concurrent Programming.

* When not to use it? The costs

- *Performance:* Every Map and Filter step creates a new slice. Calls through a function value are hard to inline for the compiler.
- *Debugging:* Long chains of anonymous functions give unreadable stack traces. Where do you set the breakpoint?
- *Readability:* Deeply nested closures are harder to read than a simple loop
- *Hidden* *state:* Closures are not automatically pure. `intSeq` has hidden state.

The Go way: Use functions as parameters where they configure behavior (sort, handlers, options). For simple processing of a slice, write a loop.

: Rob Pike hat mit robpike/filter Map/Filter/Reduce für Go gebaut. Im README steht sinngemäß: Ich habe es nie benutzt, ich nehme einfach for-Schleifen. Ihr solltet es auch nicht benutzen.
: https://github.com/robpike/filter
: Das ist auch der Grund, warum die Standardbibliothek keine Streams-API hat.

* Summary

- Functions are values: they can be stored, passed and returned
- Closures capture variables, not values
- Pure functions depend only on their arguments and have no side effects
- Two core ideas: behavior as a parameter, and pure functions. Together they make parallel and distributed execution possible (MapReduce).
- Lambda calculus: everything can be built from functions alone
- In Go: use it for behavior (sort, handlers, options), use loops for simple processing

Today: Functional programming as a tool in an impure language.
Next lecture: Haskell, a language where every function is pure.

: * Summary
: - You can do functional programming with Go
: - Generics and type inference for functions exist since Go 1.18
: - Type definitions for functions make code readable
: - You can use functional patterns and generic programming with extra casting (type assertions)
: - Functional patterns like Map/Filter/Reduce are easy to implement in Go
: - Reflection can help to avoid casting, but it is slow!
